1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,010
Interned in India:

2
00:00:02,090 --> 00:00:07,200
Singapore's Japanese
Civilians, 1941 to 1946

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,743
In the early hours of
the 8th of December 1941,

4
00:00:10,823 --> 00:00:16,678
as Japanese bombs fell on Pearl Harbor
and Singapore, the police in Singapore

5
00:00:16,758 --> 00:00:21,075
began rounding up every
Japanese resident on the island.

6
00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:24,269
Shopkeepers, photographers,
dentists, bank clerks,

7
00:00:24,349 --> 00:00:28,001
fishermen and their families
were taken from their homes,

8
00:00:28,081 --> 00:00:31,733
and within weeks most of them
were on their way to India,

9
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:36,054
where about 2,700 civilians from
Singapore, Malaya and the rest of

10
00:00:36,134 --> 00:00:40,375
British Asia spent the war behind
the walls of a sixteenth-century

11
00:00:40,455 --> 00:00:43,925
fort in Delhi and then in
a desert camp in Rajasthan.

12
00:00:43,925 --> 00:00:48,450
Few of them were ever allowed to
make Singapore their home again.

13
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:52,737
Fukuda Kurahachi had come
to Singapore in 1916,

14
00:00:52,817 --> 00:00:56,268
at the age of 13, to work at Echigoya,

15
00:00:56,348 --> 00:01:00,250
a Japanese textile store that sold kimono.

16
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:04,000
By 1941 he was its manager.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,609
With war expected, he had
planned to leave for Japan at

18
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:11,364
the end of the year, and on
Sunday, the 7th of December,

19
00:01:11,444 --> 00:01:15,200
he closed the business and
held a small farewell dinner.

20
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,549
In the diary he kept
through his internment,

21
00:01:18,629 --> 00:01:23,150
he recorded that the raids
began at 3.15 the next morning.

22
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:26,000
He was arrested at 6 am.

23
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,125
The Straits Times reported
the round-up the same day.

24
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:36,114
Japanese women and children surrendered
to the immigration and police authorities,

25
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:39,450
while the men were
rounded up at about 5 am.

26
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:43,441
About 400 were at the
immigration depot by the morning,

27
00:01:43,521 --> 00:01:49,139
where they were examined, labelled and
numbered, and the paper expected 1,300

28
00:01:49,219 --> 00:01:54,400
Japanese in Singapore to have been
taken by the time it went to press.

29
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,138
The colonial government had been
planning this since at least 1940,

30
00:01:59,218 --> 00:02:02,662
and in September 1941 the
Governor of the Straits

31
00:02:02,742 --> 00:02:07,121
Settlements had asked the
Government of India whether it would

32
00:02:07,201 --> 00:02:11,508
take Malaya's Japanese internees
if war came, about 2,000 men

33
00:02:11,588 --> 00:02:15,975
and 750 women, with the Malayan
governments paying the costs.

34
00:02:15,975 --> 00:02:20,581
The men were taken to the immigration
depot at Singapore's East Wharf

35
00:02:20,661 --> 00:02:25,538
and shipped on the SS Klang to the
quarantine station at Port Swettenham,

36
00:02:25,618 --> 00:02:29,625
the port for Kuala Lumpur,
arriving on the 9th of December.

37
00:02:29,625 --> 00:02:33,005
Eleven days later, as the
Japanese army advanced

38
00:02:33,085 --> 00:02:36,032
down the peninsula, they were brought back

39
00:02:36,112 --> 00:02:39,500
on the SS Perak and locked
up in Changi Prison.

40
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:45,161
The women and children were taken to the
quarantine station on Saint John's Island,

41
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:48,700
where they slept in long
sheds and cooked outside.

42
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:53,325
A British missionary, Norah Inge,
was appointed to guard them.

43
00:02:53,325 --> 00:02:56,764
Almost all of them were
Japanese by nationality,

44
00:02:56,844 --> 00:02:59,850
and that is how the British treated them.

45
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:05,123
There was no Singapore citizenship
until 1957, and when Japan went

46
00:03:05,203 --> 00:03:10,800
to war with Britain its nationals
here became enemy aliens overnight.

47
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,612
The round-up also took in
people from Japan's empire:

48
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:18,651
Taiwanese and Koreans, who
were then Japanese subjects,

49
00:03:18,731 --> 00:03:21,375
as well as a few Chinese and Malays.

50
00:03:21,375 --> 00:03:26,137
When the camp in India
was counted in May 1943,

51
00:03:26,217 --> 00:03:31,392
its 2,101 internees were
made up of 1,889 Japanese,

52
00:03:31,472 --> 00:03:36,234
including Okinawans, 184
Taiwanese, 10 Chinese,

53
00:03:36,314 --> 00:03:40,950
7 Thais, 5 Malays, 5
Koreans and one Russian.

54
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:46,275
The Malays were mostly women,
probably the partners of Japanese men.

55
00:03:46,275 --> 00:03:51,125
Many of them had lived in Singapore
and Malaya for a long time.

56
00:03:51,125 --> 00:03:55,116
Official estimates put
the number of Japanese

57
00:03:55,196 --> 00:03:58,734
in Malaya at about 3,600 in August 1941,

58
00:03:58,814 --> 00:04:02,975
after many families had
left ahead of the war.

59
00:04:02,975 --> 00:04:07,060
They ran photographic studios,
chemists, hotels and shops

60
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:10,859
around Middle Road, which
was known as Little Japan,

61
00:04:10,939 --> 00:04:15,536
and the internee lists in India
show more than 70 photographers,

62
00:04:15,616 --> 00:04:20,000
more than 60 barbers and
hairdressers and about 45 dentists.

63
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,637
The largest single group
was more than 400 Okinawan

64
00:04:23,717 --> 00:04:28,011
fishermen who had worked out
of Singapore, part of the fleet

65
00:04:28,091 --> 00:04:32,100
described in an earlier post
on the Japanese fishermen.

66
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:36,216
There were also managers from
the Yokohama Specie Bank,

67
00:04:36,296 --> 00:04:39,500
the Bank of Taiwan, Mitsui and Mitsubishi.

68
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,645
Fukuda had been educated at
the Anglo-Chinese School,

69
00:04:43,725 --> 00:04:47,231
and his firm was the
only Japanese company in

70
00:04:47,311 --> 00:04:51,775
Singapore to give money to
the British War Fund in 1941.

71
00:04:51,775 --> 00:04:55,493
The historians who have studied
the camp lists note that

72
00:04:55,573 --> 00:04:59,155
new generations had been
born here, though the records

73
00:04:59,235 --> 00:05:03,100
do not say how many of the
internees were Singapore-born.

74
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:07,168
On the 7th of January 1942
the men in Changi were put

75
00:05:07,248 --> 00:05:11,475
on a steamer without being
told where they were going.

76
00:05:11,475 --> 00:05:15,928
They reached Bombay on the 19th
of January and were taken north in

77
00:05:16,008 --> 00:05:20,668
third-class railway carriages to
Delhi, where the Government of India

78
00:05:20,748 --> 00:05:25,350
had prepared a temporary camp inside
the Purana Qila, the Old Fort.

79
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:29,049
The women and children sailed
separately, guessing from the

80
00:05:29,129 --> 00:05:33,100
ship's southward course that
they might be going to Australia.

81
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:35,750
They landed at Calcutta.

82
00:05:35,750 --> 00:05:39,612
One child died on the voyage,
which took almost two weeks,

83
00:05:39,692 --> 00:05:42,750
and another was born on
the wharf on arrival.

84
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:48,286
They were joined by Japanese
and others from India,

85
00:05:48,366 --> 00:05:51,370
Burma, Ceylon and even Iran,

86
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:56,435
and at its fullest the
camp held 2,689 people:

87
00:05:56,515 --> 00:06:00,700
1,718 men, 725 women and 246 children.

88
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:05,466
The Japanese consuls from across
British Asia were not interned with

89
00:06:05,546 --> 00:06:10,250
them but kept under house arrest
in the hill station of Mussoorie.

90
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:13,506
The Purana Qila was meant
to be a transit camp,

91
00:06:13,586 --> 00:06:18,119
so the men lived in about 300 army
tents, six to eight to a tent,

92
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:21,454
while the women and
children were housed in the

93
00:06:21,534 --> 00:06:25,225
arcades along the fort's
walls, divided by curtains.

94
00:06:25,225 --> 00:06:28,722
The internees organised
themselves almost at once,

95
00:06:28,802 --> 00:06:33,300
electing councils and wing leaders
and setting up night patrols,

96
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:37,100
kitchen shifts, a tailoring
department and a barber.

97
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:41,299
A baseball season began on
the 20th of February 1942,

98
00:06:41,379 --> 00:06:44,771
and baseball scores
are, after the weather,

99
00:06:44,851 --> 00:06:49,050
the subject Fukuda recorded
most often in his diary.

100
00:06:49,050 --> 00:06:51,975
The weather was the first hardship.

101
00:06:51,975 --> 00:06:56,237
The internees had come from the
tropics into a Delhi winter,

102
00:06:56,317 --> 00:07:00,796
and a Thai internee, Karuna
Kusalasaya, later remembered hands,

103
00:07:00,876 --> 00:07:04,350
feet and lips cracked and
blistered by the cold.

104
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:08,254
By June the temperature
in the tents reached

105
00:07:08,334 --> 00:07:12,500
115 degrees Fahrenheit,
or 46 degrees Celsius.

106
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:17,571
Disease followed: dysentery and
malaria in the monsoon months,

107
00:07:17,651 --> 00:07:20,147
and from October 1942 beriberi,

108
00:07:20,227 --> 00:07:23,800
caused by a lack of
vitamin B1 in the diet.

109
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:29,556
By the end of 1942, 106
internees had died in the camp,

110
00:07:29,636 --> 00:07:33,350
55 men, 42 women and nine children.

111
00:07:33,350 --> 00:07:38,389
One was a 14-year-old boy, George
Sakai, who died of appendicitis,

112
00:07:38,469 --> 00:07:42,425
and the internees named a
baseball prize after him.

113
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:45,358
Japan's government
protested about the tents,

114
00:07:45,438 --> 00:07:48,572
the food and the sanitation
through Switzerland,

115
00:07:48,652 --> 00:07:52,000
the neutral power that
looked after its interests.

116
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,196
The authorities in India answered
that the camp was the same as

117
00:07:56,276 --> 00:08:00,812
the accommodation given to Indian
troops, but in London one official

118
00:08:00,892 --> 00:08:05,100
wrote privately that tents in
India were "hardly good enough".

119
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:08,296
The British authorities
then added barley to

120
00:08:08,376 --> 00:08:11,950
the rations and enlarged
the medical facilities.

121
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:16,368
In mid-1942 Britain and
Japan agreed to exchange some

122
00:08:16,448 --> 00:08:20,357
of the civilians each
held, at Lourenço Marques

123
00:08:20,437 --> 00:08:24,850
in Portuguese East Africa,
now Maputo in Mozambique.

124
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:29,138
The exchange of August
and September 1942 sent

125
00:08:29,218 --> 00:08:34,551
about 1,800 Japanese home from
across the British Empire,

126
00:08:34,631 --> 00:08:38,525
including about 720 from the Purana Qila.

127
00:08:38,525 --> 00:08:41,826
The list was drawn up
by the Japanese consuls,

128
00:08:41,906 --> 00:08:47,125
a committee of camp leaders and the
officials negotiating the exchange.

129
00:08:47,125 --> 00:08:51,068
Consular staff came first,
then the managers of the banks,

130
00:08:51,148 --> 00:08:55,715
rubber companies, newspapers and
trading houses and their families,

131
00:08:55,795 --> 00:08:58,500
then teachers and the sick and elderly.

132
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:01,115
Fukuda was number

133
00:09:01,195 --> 00:09:04,525
480 on a list of 784.

134
00:09:04,525 --> 00:09:08,350
There was not a fisherman
or a tailor on the list.

135
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:11,738
Some of the Okinawans and
Taiwanese who were left behind

136
00:09:11,818 --> 00:09:14,464
attacked members of the
selection committee,

137
00:09:14,544 --> 00:09:18,490
accusing them of choosing only
their own class, and in the nights

138
00:09:18,570 --> 00:09:22,100
before the departure some of
those leaving hid from them.

139
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:27,205
Fukuda went back to Singapore,
now Syonan, in September 1942,

140
00:09:27,285 --> 00:09:31,875
working for the occupation
forces as a buyer of goods.

141
00:09:31,875 --> 00:09:35,696
In March and April 1943,
after a year of delays,

142
00:09:35,776 --> 00:09:39,273
the remaining internees,
about 2,100 people,

143
00:09:39,353 --> 00:09:44,719
were moved to permanent barracks
at Deoli, near Ajmer in Rajasthan,

144
00:09:44,799 --> 00:09:48,700
which had been used for
German prisoners of war.

145
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:52,849
The barracks were whitewashed
brick with stone-tiled roofs,

146
00:09:52,929 --> 00:09:57,875
about 700 internees to each wing,
with barbed wire between the wings.

147
00:09:57,875 --> 00:10:00,400
Conditions were better.

148
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:03,076
The camp had a hospital with Japanese,

149
00:10:03,156 --> 00:10:08,371
European and Indian doctors, a school
for the children with ten teachers,

150
00:10:08,451 --> 00:10:11,998
sports, religious services
and a lake nearby where

151
00:10:12,078 --> 00:10:15,850
internees could go to fish
and bathe, 100 at a time.

152
00:10:15,850 --> 00:10:19,269
Visitors from the
International Committee of the

153
00:10:19,349 --> 00:10:22,842
Red Cross counted about
230 rock gardens that the

154
00:10:22,922 --> 00:10:26,275
internees had made in
front of their quarters.

155
00:10:26,275 --> 00:10:32,400
A second exchange was negotiated, and
934 names were put forward from Deoli,

156
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:36,725
but after several postponements
it never took place.

157
00:10:36,725 --> 00:10:39,643
The internees were still at Deoli

158
00:10:39,723 --> 00:10:43,175
when Japan surrendered in August 1945.

159
00:10:43,175 --> 00:10:46,050
The end of the war split the camp.

160
00:10:46,050 --> 00:10:49,983
Many internees refused to
believe that Japan had lost

161
00:10:50,063 --> 00:10:54,000
and accused the British of
printing fake newspapers.

162
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,808
They became known as the
kachi-gumi, the "victory group",

163
00:10:57,888 --> 00:11:00,605
and they turned on the smaller make-gumi,

164
00:11:00,685 --> 00:11:03,550
the "defeat group", who accepted the news.

165
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:07,668
On the 20th of February
1946 about 80 internees

166
00:11:07,748 --> 00:11:11,500
asked the camp authorities for protection.

167
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:15,397
When the commandant then tried
to arrest the ringleaders,

168
00:11:15,477 --> 00:11:20,500
internees in one wing drove out the
staff and refused to take roll call.

169
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:23,824
Troops were called in, and on the 26th

170
00:11:23,904 --> 00:11:26,950
of February 1946 they opened fire.

171
00:11:26,950 --> 00:11:30,344
A Reuters report at the
time described a riot

172
00:11:30,424 --> 00:11:34,126
by internees who did not
believe in the surrender

173
00:11:34,206 --> 00:11:37,525
and said 15 had been
killed and 16 wounded.

174
00:11:37,525 --> 00:11:41,650
Later Japanese accounts put
the final toll at 19 dead,

175
00:11:41,730 --> 00:11:45,310
including women and a
child, though the figures

176
00:11:45,390 --> 00:11:49,050
in different sources
range from 14 to about 20.

177
00:11:49,050 --> 00:11:54,658
Japan's government sent a message to
the camp through the Swedish legation,

178
00:11:54,738 --> 00:11:58,071
with extracts from the
Emperor's broadcast of

179
00:11:58,151 --> 00:12:02,550
the 15th of August 1945, to
confirm that the war was over.

180
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:06,532
The internees finally
left Deoli in May 1946,

181
00:12:06,612 --> 00:12:09,500
nine months after the surrender.

182
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:13,150
They were not allowed
to go home to Singapore.

183
00:12:13,150 --> 00:12:17,597
An official notice in January
1946 had announced that Japanese,

184
00:12:17,677 --> 00:12:20,688
Korean and Taiwanese
civilians would not be

185
00:12:20,768 --> 00:12:24,640
permitted to remain in Malaya
and would be evacuated in

186
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:28,736
the same way as Japanese
troops, and that no applications

187
00:12:28,816 --> 00:12:32,625
to stay on compassionate
grounds would be considered.

188
00:12:32,625 --> 00:12:37,037
The returning internees were held
in Singapore while they waited to

189
00:12:37,117 --> 00:12:41,395
be shipped on, the Japanese in a
camp at Jurong and the Taiwanese

190
00:12:41,475 --> 00:12:45,900
at Bukit Timah, and were then sent
to Japan, Taiwan and elsewhere.

191
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:48,175
The bill came later.

192
00:12:48,175 --> 00:12:52,316
In May 1951 India presented
the Malayan governments with

193
00:12:52,396 --> 00:12:56,235
a claim for 12.7 million
rupees, about $8.1 million,

194
00:12:56,315 --> 00:13:01,134
for keeping the Japanese internees,
and the Indian Home Minister,

195
00:13:01,214 --> 00:13:04,299
C. Rajagopalachari, told Parliament in New

196
00:13:04,379 --> 00:13:08,600
Delhi that he hoped the amount
would be "received soon".

197
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:13,608
Malaya argued that Britain
should pay, since the internees

198
00:13:13,688 --> 00:13:17,030
had been sent to India at its instance,

199
00:13:17,110 --> 00:13:23,171
but in September 1951 the Finance
Committee of Singapore's Legislative

200
00:13:23,251 --> 00:13:27,550
Council approved $4,644,972
as Singapore's share.

201
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:31,117
The committee's statement
said that about 2,400 Japanese

202
00:13:31,197 --> 00:13:35,285
nationals had been transferred
from Malaya to India, that it had

203
00:13:35,365 --> 00:13:39,127
been agreed in 1941 that the
Malayan governments would meet

204
00:13:39,207 --> 00:13:43,100
all the expenses, and that the
claim would be split according

205
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:46,421
to how many internees had
come from each territory:

206
00:13:46,501 --> 00:13:51,125
three-fifths for Singapore and
two-fifths for the Federation of Malaya.

207
00:13:51,125 --> 00:13:54,375
Some of the internees did come back.

208
00:13:54,375 --> 00:13:59,367
Fukuda returned to Singapore
in 1954, and in November

209
00:13:59,447 --> 00:14:03,196
1955 he reopened Echigoya in a renovated

210
00:14:03,276 --> 00:14:08,555
three-storey building on
Coleman Street, as Echigoya and

211
00:14:08,635 --> 00:14:13,325
Co. 1955 Limited, with
$250,000 of local capital.

212
00:14:13,325 --> 00:14:16,962
The Singapore Standard called
it the first Japanese firm

213
00:14:17,042 --> 00:14:20,546
to operate in Malaya after
the war with local capital,

214
00:14:20,626 --> 00:14:24,395
and noted that Fukuda had lived
in Singapore for nearly 40

215
00:14:24,475 --> 00:14:28,245
years and that, when the war
broke out, he had surrendered

216
00:14:28,325 --> 00:14:31,975
the company's assets to the
British authorities intact.

217
00:14:31,975 --> 00:14:36,575
He ran the business
until it closed in 1977.

218
00:14:36,575 --> 00:14:40,521
Why it matters today: The
internment of Singapore's Japanese

219
00:14:40,601 --> 00:14:44,816
residents was largely forgotten
in all three countries involved:

220
00:14:44,896 --> 00:14:47,702
in Britain, beside the
suffering of its own

221
00:14:47,782 --> 00:14:52,063
prisoners of war; in India, beside
the struggle for independence;

222
00:14:52,143 --> 00:14:55,700
and in Japan, where the
internees seldom spoke of it.

223
00:14:55,700 --> 00:14:59,983
When it happened, no international
treaty set rules for civilians

224
00:15:00,063 --> 00:15:04,481
held in wartime, and the warring
powers broadly agreed to apply the

225
00:15:04,561 --> 00:15:08,777
relevant parts of the 1929 Geneva
Convention on prisoners of war

226
00:15:08,857 --> 00:15:13,408
to them instead; in Delhi the
authorities admitted that the makeshift

227
00:15:13,488 --> 00:15:17,650
camp meant even those principles
could not be applied in full.

228
00:15:17,650 --> 00:15:22,308
The Fourth Geneva Convention
of 1949 now sets out how enemy

229
00:15:22,388 --> 00:15:28,090
civilians may be interned and how
they must be housed, fed and released,

230
00:15:28,170 --> 00:15:32,025
and Singapore has been a
party to it since 1973.

231
00:15:32,025 --> 00:15:35,506
The internment of Fukuda
and his fellow residents

232
00:15:35,586 --> 00:15:39,075
shows the gap those rules
were written to close.
